\paragraph{Identity replacement and the B3--R3 gap (exploratory follow-up).} D2+H3 is D2 plus one switch $x$ that lets the attacker leave after a failed audit and return under a new identity. At the headline market, the D2+H3 search leaves \site{rs_hl_b3} more gain against B3 than the D2 search, and \site{rs_hl_r3} more against R3, so the B3 minus R3 gap is \site{rs_hl_g1} under D2+H3 and \site{rs_hl_g0} under D2. To see whether this holds on the six fresh markets, we ran after the report the D2 cells that budget cut~\const{3} had removed (B3, R3, and C$^*$ at $\rho=\const{0.2}$, each with a doubling check), under a plan and expectations written before any fresh D2 result existed, but after the headline contrast and the six fresh-market D2+H3 outcomes had been seen. This is a descriptive follow-up, not an independent replication, and it changes no registered decision. Pooled over the six markets with equal weights, the B3 minus R3 gap is \site{rs_g1} (\const{95}\% paired seed-bootstrap interval [\site{rs_g1_lo}, \site{rs_g1_hi}]) under D2+H3 and \site{rs_g0} ([\site{rs_g0_lo}, \site{rs_g0_hi}]) under D2 (doubled searches: \site{rs_g1_d} and \site{rs_g0_d}). The share of the gap that disappears without replacement, $S = \const{1} - G(\mathrm{D2})/G(\mathrm{D2{+}H3})$, is \site{rs_s} (doubled searches: \site{rs_s_d}). It exceeds one because the pooled D2 gap is slightly negative. Its paired-seed interval excludes zero conditional on these markets and selected policies, whereas the exploratory $t$ interval across the six market means, [\site{rs_gd0_t_lo}, \site{rs_gd0_t_hi}], includes zero; the gap is positive on \site{rs_gd0_pos} of the six markets (\site{rs_gd0_pos_d} with the doubled searches), and most of its negative value comes from fresh5 (without fresh5, $S$ is \site{rs_s_loo5}). We therefore do not establish a negative average gap across the market neighbourhood. The seed intervals above hold the six markets fixed; leaving out one market at a time gives $S$ from \site{rs_loo_lo} to \site{rs_loo_hi} over both search versions, and Table~\ref{tab:ph-reset} lists the gaps per market. The same holds for the Q1 contrast: the pooled C$^*$ minus R3 gain difference is \site{rs_cr1} under D2+H3 and \site{rs_cr0} under D2 ($t$ interval [\site{rs_cr0_lo}, \site{rs_cr0_hi}]; doubled searches [\site{rs_cr0_dlo}, \site{rs_cr0_dhi}]). The difference between the two prescribed searches, $I = g(\mathrm{D2{+}H3}) - g(\mathrm{D2})$, is \site{rs_i_b3} against B3, \site{rs_i_r3} against R3, and \site{rs_i_c} against C$^*$; it does not identify the optimal value of replacement. The expectations set in advance score as follows. $I(\mathrm{B3}) > I(\mathrm{R3})$ on every market: ``\site{rs_exp_b}'' (\site{rs_sign_b} markets with the frozen searches, \site{rs_sign_bd} with the doubled ones). $I(\mathrm{C}^*) > I(\mathrm{R3})$ on every market: ``\site{rs_exp_c}'' (\site{rs_sign_c} and \site{rs_sign_cd} markets; fresh3 gives \site{rs_f3_c} with the frozen searches). Pooled $S > \const{0.5}$: ``\site{rs_exp_s}''. Within the D2 family, at the fixed parameters and search budgets, most of the estimated B3 minus R3 gap under the prescribed searches disappears when the attacker cannot replace its identity. The two searched winners can differ in every knob, not only $x$. With every other knob held fixed, switching replacement off in the D2+H3 winner lowers the gain by \site{rs_fx_b3} against B3, \site{rs_fx_r3} against R3, and \site{rs_fx_c} against C$^*$, and switching it on in the D2 winner raises the gain by only \site{rs_rev_b3}, \site{rs_rev_r3}, and \site{rs_rev_c}. No D2 winner replaces its identity. The truthful references can: \site{rs_ref_any} of the \site{rs_ref_n} frozen references replace an identity on the evaluation seeds. Each rule's reference cancels in $I$ but not in $G$. As a sensitivity calculation, we measured on the Mac each frozen reference's profit with replacement and with replacement disabled, and added the signed difference to the stored Linux gains; the adjustment can increase or decrease a gain, and it is not a re-evaluation with non-replacing references. The largest absolute difference is \site{rs_ref_max}; the adjustment moves G(D2+H3) and G(D2) by the same amount, less than \const{0.001}, leaves $S$ at \site{rs_ref_s}, and moves the C$^*$ minus R3 difference by less than \const{0.002}. The Mac reproduces the stored reference profits only on average, with a largest absolute mean discrepancy of about \site{rs_ref_val}, close to the largest replacement difference. $S$ is a ratio of two searched gaps and carries their search error (Table~\ref{tab:ph-reset}). \site{rs_flag} of the \site{rs_flag_n} D2 doubling checks are flagged (largest change \site{rs_flag_max}), and on fresh0 the D2 gap moves from \site{rs_f0_g0} to \site{rs_f0_g0_d} under doubling; an unflagged cell is not a converged one. In two frozen comparisons the evaluated D2+H3 winner earns less than the evaluated D2 winner: $I$ is \site{rs_nest_c} for C$^*$ on fresh3 and \site{rs_nest_r} for R3 on fresh4. These reversals can reflect finite search or differences between training and evaluation outcomes; they disappear under doubling, which does not establish convergence. We keep the frozen searches as primary and report the doubled results as sensitivity.
\begin{table}[t]\centering\small
\adjustbox{max width=\linewidth}{\input{tables/posthoc_reset.tex}}
\caption{Exploratory follow-up, after the report: the difference between the two prescribed searches, $I = g(\mathrm{D2{+}H3}) - g(\mathrm{D2})$, per rule, its contrasts, the B3 minus R3 gaps $G$ under each family, and the share $S$, pooled over the six fresh markets with equal weights, with paired seed-bootstrap percentile intervals; frozen and doubled searches. $S$ is shown only when the lower bound of the pooled D2+H3 gap exceeds the preset floor. The lower block gives the gaps per market (D2+H3 / D2).}\label{tab:ph-reset}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Honest identity churn (exploratory follow-up).} In the registered markets every honest provider keeps one identity for the whole episode, so the newest identities are mostly the attacker's. After the report we let each of the nine honest opponents retire its identity and re-enter under a fresh one with probability $p$ in each round, $p = \const{0.01}$ or $p = \const{0.05}$ (mean identity lifetimes of \const{100} and \const{20} rounds; \site{ch_reps1} and \site{ch_reps5} honest replacements per episode), and reran the registered D2+H3 search, evaluation, and doubling check for B3, R3, and C$^*$ on the six fresh markets at $\rho=\const{0.2}$, each against the rule's frozen truthful reference played under the same churn. The rates are illustrative sensitivity settings, not calibrated to any market. The plan and four expectations were written before any churn search, after a timing check on one market (one rule, the old attack, no reference) had been seen. The churn model keeps capacity and quality fixed, but it also resets an honest provider's history and lets it leave a false ban, so it does not isolate age information. This is an exploratory follow-up; it changes no registered decision, and its intervals hold the six markets and the selected policies fixed. At $p = \const{0.05}$, the pooled gain against R3 rises from \site{ch_r3_0} to \site{ch_r3_5} (paired seed-bootstrap interval [\site{ch_r3_5_lo}, \site{ch_r3_5_hi}]), while the gains against C$^*$ and B3 change much less (from \site{ch_c_0} to \site{ch_c_5}, and from \site{ch_b_0} to \site{ch_b_5}). The C$^*$ minus R3 difference falls from \site{ch_d_0} to \site{ch_d_5} ([\site{ch_d_5_lo}, \site{ch_d_5_hi}]; negative on \site{ch_d_5_neg} of the six markets), and the B3 minus R3 gap becomes \site{ch_q_5} ([\site{ch_q_5_lo}, \site{ch_q_5_hi}]; negative on \site{ch_q_5_neg} markets): under this churn, the attack earns more against R3 than against the rule without audit priority. The rise comes from the attack's profit, not from a weaker reference: against R3, the attack's mean profit rises by \site{ch_pa_5} and the reference's by \site{ch_pr_5} ([\site{ch_pr_5_lo}, \site{ch_pr_5_hi}]). At $p = \const{0.01}$, the C$^*$ minus R3 difference is \site{ch_d_1} ([\site{ch_d_1_lo}, \site{ch_d_1_hi}]; doubled searches \site{ch_d_1_d}), and the B3 minus R3 gap stays at \site{ch_q_1}. The diagnostics show how. At $p = \const{0.05}$, over the rounds in which the attacker holds assigned jobs, its identity is the newest among those holding assigned jobs, the order in which R3 ranks identities, in \site{ch_new_r3} of the rounds under R3 (\site{ch_new_c} under C$^*$, \site{ch_new_b} under B3). R3 audits \site{ch_sh_r3} of the jobs that honest replacement identities serve in their first ten rounds, against \site{ch_sh_c} under C$^*$ and \site{ch_sh_b} under B3, pooled over the markets. R3's newcomer priority thus moves audits to honest newcomers while the attacker's identity hides among them. The expectations set in advance, scored on point estimates under both the frozen and the doubled searches: pooled $g_{\const{0.05}}(\mathrm{R3})$ above its no-churn value, ``\site{ch_e1}''; pooled C$^*$ minus R3 at $p = \const{0.05}$ below its no-churn value, ``\site{ch_e2}''; B3 minus R3 positive on all six markets at $p = \const{0.01}$, ``\site{ch_e3}''; the audited share of honest replacements in their first ten rounds higher under R3 than under C$^*$ and B3 at $p = \const{0.05}$, ``\site{ch_e4}''. \site{ch_inc} of the \site{ch_inc_n} doubling checks are flagged incomplete; at $p = \const{0.05}$ the doubled searches give the same sign for every pooled gain and contrast, while at $p = \const{0.01}$ the C$^*$ minus R3 difference changes sign under doubling (Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn}). Within this model, at the higher rate, R3's measured advantage over C$^*$ and B3 does not survive honest churn.

\begin{table}[t]\centering\small
\adjustbox{max width=\linewidth}{\input{tables/posthoc_churn.tex}}
\caption{Exploratory follow-up, after the report: the D2+H3 gain $g$ against R3, C$^*$, and B3, and the contrasts $d$ = C$^*$ minus R3 and $q$ = B3 minus R3, pooled over the six fresh markets with equal weights, when each honest opponent replaces its identity with probability $p$ per round; $p$ = \const{0} is the registered no-churn result on the same seeds. Paired seed-bootstrap percentile intervals; frozen and doubled searches.}\label{tab:ph-churn}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Which parts of R3 matter (Q2).} Under separately searched D2+H3 attacks, pooled over the fresh markets, the main effects on the gain are \site{r_q2_p} for the priority phase P and \site{r_q2_g} for newest-first residual targeting G, both reductions, and \site{r_q2_s} for pacing S, a small increase (X7-SC: \site{r_q2_s_x}). The two targeting parts are substitutes: the PG factorial contrast, half the difference between G's conditional effects, is \site{r_q2_pg}; G changes the gain by \site{r_q2_g_p0} without the priority phase and by only \site{r_q2_g_p1} with it, and the priority phase changes it by only \site{r_q2_p_g1} once G is in place (averaged over both pacing settings). R3's own comparison, $F_{111}$ minus $F_{011}$ (exploratory, from the evaluation records), is \site{r_dp} pooled: exactly zero on four markets, where both rules leave the attacker the same gain, and nonzero on fresh1 (\site{r_dp_f1}) and fresh3. Against the fixed attacks, the priority phase's effect given newest-first targeting is larger (\site{r_q2_fixed_pg1} under D2+H3 and \site{r_q2_fixed_pg1_x} under X7-SC, averaged over pacing), though still small next to newest-first targeting's effect without the priority phase (above). In the pooled, separately searched comparisons, given newest-first targeting the priority phase adds little at these audit ratios and capacities; at a lower audit ratio or a larger attacker capacity, half of a round's audits may no longer cover one provider's jobs, and the substitution need not hold. In the pooled contrasts, either P or G alone accounts for most of the measured reduction; adding the other contributes little. The pattern also appears in the fixed-attack contrasts averaged over the eight selected attacks (P: \site{r_q2_fixed_p}). Our registered conjectures (P negligible, the targeting parts additive, S helpful) failed as scored (Table~\ref{tab:predictions}); the stricter eligibility reading of Table~\ref{tab:pred-eligibility} leaves them not assessed, with the estimates unchanged. X7-SC agrees (Table~\ref{tab:q2-x7sc}).
